## Question 3 — pick **3C: keep the envelope, add a reconciling clause**

**Reasoning.** 3B cannot be built: a lifecycle event has no payload to carry fields and a scalar payload has no fields to carry correlation, so "fields on the ordinary value" either constrains the wire or wraps — an envelope under another name, differing only in what it is called. The authority's sentence, read in its paragraph, forbids *semantic vocabulary and protocol machinery* — a taxonomy of what values mean, version fields, medium-owned framing — not data-shaped provenance. Both contents of this map are blessed elsewhere in the same authority: correlation by identity in the data is the mechanism Host Boundaries itself names, and classifying host occurrences into qualified values is the transform's *mandated* duty ("a transform function classifies them and emits a qualified value"). `:ws/event` closes over what the socket itself did — opened, closed, erred, disclaimed — never what a value means, and the adapter never inspects `:ws/value`, so deposit-everything-judge-nothing is intact. A per-attachment deposit medium would remove multiplexing attribution but not the lifecycle/payload distinction, not the boundary-level announcement stream's own identity problem, and not ADR 0003's rejection of the private-ingress shape — it is a legal composition choice (which the ws spec already permits), not the answer. Since the envelope does not violate the authority, the subordinate may document that reading at the point of use; amending `datom.world.md` for one transport's need would spend constitutional authority where nothing in it is actually contradicted.

**Exact text for `dao.stream.ws.md`** (replace the "**Deposited events are envelopes**…" paragraph and its code block with):

> **Deposited events are maps, each carrying three transport-owned facts and judging nothing:**
>
> ```clojure
> {:dao.stream/attachment <id>
>  :ws/event              :ws/payload  ; or :ws/opened :ws/closed :ws/error
>                                      ;    :ws/not-found :ws/transport-error
>  :ws/value              <decoded>}   ; payload events only
> ```
>
> This is not the bespoke envelope, version field, or fact taxonomy that `datom.world.md` excludes, and the distinction is load-bearing. `:dao.stream/attachment` is correlation by identity in the data — the mechanism the authority itself names. `:ws/event` is the transform's classification of what the socket itself did, the same duty the authority already imposes on host error types; it closes over occurrences, never meanings, and its `not-found`/`transport-error` classes are the displaced `attach!` outcomes of the contract, whose distinction survives with only its channel moved. No medium-owned framing exists and the adapter never inspects `:ws/value`: strip the `:ws/…` keys and the payload stands as itself. A map is also the only honest shape — a lifecycle event has no payload to carry fields, and a scalar payload has no fields to carry correlation — so under a per-attachment deposit medium the attribution key is redundant but harmless, and under one medium per boundary it is the demultiplexing key.

